{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":7524,"output":2069,"cacheRead":22400,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1988,"totalTokens":31993,"cost":{"input":0.0011286,"output":0.0010345,"cacheRead":0.000672,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0028351}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There's no existing scenario→tool mapping in src. In main.rs, I need a `scenario_tool_of` closure. The scenario names prefix-encode the tool (\"wai-hint-adherence-*\" → wai, \"dont-*\" → dont). But I should keep it simple and derive from scenario name prefix: if name starts with \"wai-\" → \"wai\", if starts with \"dont-\" → \"dont\". Or better: check the scenario registry. Actually routing.rs says \"an unknown scenario is a loud error\". Simplest defensible implementation: a helper in report.rs or main.rs that maps scenario name prefix. But wait — the brief says report.rs calls routing::propose. So the aggregation/routing wiring should live in report.rs (library code, testable), with main.rs just passing flags.\n\nLet me design:\n\n**report.rs additions:**\n- `pub fn scenario_tool_of(name: &str) -> Option<String>` — derive from scenario definition prefix? Hmm. Actually the routing.rs doc says \"key the target repository on the scenario definition's recorded tool\". The scenario definitions in src/scenario.rs are wai-* or dont-*. Maybe add a helper in report.rs:\n\n```rust\npub fn scenario_tool_of(name: &str) -> Option<String> {\n    if name.starts_with(\"wai-\") { Some(\"wai\".into()) }\n    else if name.starts_with(\"dont-\") { Some(\"dont\".into()) }\n    else { None }\n}\n```\n\n- `pub fn route_report(dashboard: &mut serde_json::Value, rows, configured, repo_map) -> Result<(), String>` or a function `attach_routing(dashboard, rows, configured_models, repo_map) -> Result<RoutedSummary, String>`:\n\nActually brief says: report.rs — call routing::propose after aggregation when --route; attach `routed` summary {filed, existing, dry_run, skipped} to the report JSON; write proposed-tickets.jsonl next to the report when routing ran.\n\nSo report.rs needs:\n- A summary tally: count decisions filed/existing/dry_run/skipped. In dry-run, filed=0, existing=0; dry_run counts mapped proposals; skipped counts unmapped. But to be future-proof (phase 2 --file), file_proposals mutates decisions to filed/existing/skipped(bd_failure). The summary counts across all decisions.\n\nDesign a function:\n\n```rust\npub struct RoutedSummary { filed, existing, dry_run, skipped }  // or just build json\n\npub fn route_and_summarize(\n    dashboard: &mut serde_json::Value,\n    rows: &[serde_json::Value],\n    configured_models: &[String],\n    repo_map: &BTreeMap<String, String>,\n    file_authorized: bool,\n    run_bd: &mut BdRunner,\n) -> Result<serde_json::Value, String>\n```\n\nHmm, but tests in tests/report_aggregation.rs need to test: routed field present after --route dry-run; report_version still 1; --file without --route is an error (CLI level — that's main.rs, testable via assert on CLI parsing? It's a runtime check in main, hard to test from integration test unless the check is in report.rs). Let me put the validation in report.rs: a function that errors if file_authorized && !route. Actually cleaner: in main.rs clap, `--file` requires `--route` — clap supports `requires` but that auto-enables. We want --file without --route → error. clap's `requires_ifs`/conflicts... Actually we want: `--file` errors unless `--route`. In clap, `#[arg(long, requires = \"route\")]` makes --route required when --file present — that gives error \"the following required arguments were not provided: --route\". That's exactly \"--file requires --route; error otherwise\". And it's testable via `Cli::try_parse`. But Cli is in main.rs (binary), not accessible from integration tests. Tests/report_aggregation.rs is an integration test against the lib. So the CLI flag test can't reach the binary's Cli struct.\n\nOptions: put the validation in report.rs as a function that main calls, e.g.:\n\n```rust\npub fn validate_route_flags(route: bool, file: bool) -> Result<(), String> {\n    if file && !route {\n        return Err(\"--file requires --route\".into());\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\nThen test: validate_route_flags(false, true).is_err(). And main.rs uses clap requires too? Keep both? Simplest: do the check in report.rs (tested) and in main.rs Report variant run_report takes (path, route, file) and errors. Actually I'll have main.rs parse flags and call report.rs functions; the check function in report.rs is the tested surface. In clap, I could use `requires = \"route\"` for nice UX but the brief says \"--file requires --route; error otherwise\". Using clap requires gives that for free, but the integration test can't test clap. So implement the check in report.rs, and don't duplicate in clap (or do both — harmless). I'll do the report.rs check only, called from main.\n\nNow the routing runner injection: file_proposals takes a BdRunner. For dry-run tests, run_bd never called when file_authorized=false. For report.rs, I need a default runner = routing::run_bd. But for testability in tests/report_aggregation.rs, tests only cover dry-run, so a spy isn't needed for the brief's tests. But routing failures (unmapped tool) → skipped counted. propose() already handles that with decision skipped/unmapped_tool.\n\nSo report.rs:\n\n```rust\npub fn route_dashboard(\n    rows: &[serde_json::Value],\n    configured_models: &[String],\n    repo_map: &BTreeMap<String, String>,\n    file_authorized: bool,\n    run_bd: &mut routing::BdRunner,\n) -> Result<serde_json::Value, String>  // the \"routed\" summary json\n```\n\nHmm, but the brief says report.rs attaches routed summary to the report JSON. Let me define:\n\n```rust\n/// Attach the additive `routed` summary to a dashboard...\npub fn attach_routing(\n    dashboard: &mut serde_json::Value,\n    rows: &[serde_json::Value],\n    configured_models: &[String],\n    repo_map: &BTreeMap<String, String>,\n    file_authorized: bool,\n    run_bd: &mut BdRunner,\n) -> Result<Vec<routing::ProposalRow>, String>\n```\n\nWait — the summary needs filed/existing/dry_run/skipped counts after file_proposals mutates decisions. So:\n\n```rust\nlet mut proposals = routing::propose(rows, configured_models, &scenario_tool_of, repo_map)?;\nrouting::file_proposals(&mut proposals, file_authorized, run_bd)?;\nlet mut filed=0; existing=0; dry_run=0; skipped=0;\nfor p in &proposals { match p.decision.as_str() { \"filed\" => filed+=1, \"existing\" => existing+=1, \"dry_run\" => dry_run+=1, _ => skipped+=1 } }\ndashboard[\"routed\"] = json!({\"filed\":.., \"existing\":.., \"dry_run\":.., \"skipped\":..});\nOk(proposals)\n```\n\nAnd main writes proposed-tickets.jsonl next to the report path when route ran: `routing::to_jsonl(&proposals)` to `<report_dir>/proposed-tickets.jsonl`.\n\n\"absent rows never routed\" — propose() already skips kind=absent. Test: rows with absent + route → routed summary with dry_run 0 and no proposals.\n\nAlso \"routing failures (unmapped tool) → skipped counted, report still succeeds\": test repo_map empty → decision skipped, summary skipped > 0, attach_routing Ok.\n\nNote propose's errors: configured_models empty → Err. In attach_routing, propagate.\n\nscenario_tool_of: needs to resolve scenarios to their recorded tool. The real scenario registry: scenario names → tools. In report.rs, implement:\n\n```rust\n/// The scenario definition's recorded tool — the repo key for routing.\n/// Scenario names prefix-encode the eval target by convention\n/// (`wai-*` / `dont-*`); an unknown prefix is None (loud error downstream).\npub fn scenario_tool_of(name: &str) -> Option<String> {\n    if name.starts_with(\"wai-\") { Some(\"wai\".into()) }\n    else if name.starts_with(\"dont-\") { Some(\"dont\".into()) }\n    else { None }\n}\n```\n\nThat's reasonable; comment explains.\n\nNow main.rs wiring:\n\n```rust\nReport {\n    path: String,\n    /// Run the fault-routing step (dry-run): attach the routed summary\n    /// and write proposed-tickets.jsonl next to the report.\n    #[arg(long)]\n    route: bool,\n    /// File proposed tickets in their mapped repos (Phase 2). Requires --route.\n    #[arg(long)]\n    file: bool,\n}\n```\n\nrun_report(&path, route, file):\n- read rows, aggregate as before\n- if route: validate flags (report::validate_route_flags), load routing.toml from repo root? Where does routing.toml live? Let me check tests/routing.rs repo_map usage and whether there's a routing.toml file in repo. grep."}}
