s if-no-files-found. Add `if-no-files-found: warn`. Good.\\n\\nNow the summary counts on proposed rows: dry_run counts mapped proposals. If the rotation had no threshold faults, proposals empty → jsonl empty file. Fine.\\n\\nImplementation details for report.rs:\\n\\n```rust\\nuse crate::routing;\\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\\n\\n/// --file without --route is a loud CLI error...\\npub fn validate_route_flags(route: bool, file: bool) -> Result<(), String> {\\n    if file && !route {\\n        return Err(\\\"--file requires --route: filing proposed tickets only makes sense after the routing step ran\\\".into());\\n    }\\n    Ok(())\\n}\\n\\n/// The scenario definition's recorded tool — the repo key for routing\\n/// (routing.rs keys proposals on this, never the row's tool field).\\n/// Scenario names prefix-encode the eval target by convention\\n/// (`wai-*`, `dont-*`); an unknown prefix is None → loud error\\n/// downstream (never a silent bucket).\\npub fn scenario_tool_of(name: &str) -> Option<String> {\\n    if name.starts_with(\\\"wai-\\\") {\\n        Some(\\\"wai\\\".to_owned())\\n    } else if name.starts_with(\\\"dont-\\\") {\\n        Some(\\\"dont\\\".to_owned())\\n    } else {\\n        None\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npub fn attach_routing(...) -> Result<Vec<routing::ProposalRow>, String> {\\n    let mut proposals = routing::propose(rows, configured_models, &scenario_tool_of, repo_map)?;\\n    routing::file_proposals(&mut proposals, file_authorized, run_bd)?;\\n    let (mut filed, mut existing, mut dry_run, mut skipped) = (0usize, 0, 0, 0);\\n    for p in &proposals {\\n        match p.decision.as_str() {\\n            \\\"filed\\\" => filed += 1,\\n            \\\"existing\\\" => existing += 1,\\n            \\\"dry_run\\\" => dry_run += 1,\\n            _ => skipped += 1,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    dashboard[\\\"routed\\\"] = json!({\\n        \\\"filed\\\": filed, \\\"existing\\\": existing, \\\"dry_run\\\": dry_run, \\\"skipped\\\": skipped,\\n    });\\n    Ok(proposals)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote dashboard is serde_json::Value object; indexing assignment `dashboard[\\\"routed\\\"] = ...` requires it to be an Object — aggregate_rows output is object, fine. But assigning to a Value via IndexMut on non-object panics; aggregate output always object. OK.\\n\\nfile_proposals signature: `run_bd: &mut BdRunner` where BdRunner = dyn FnMut(...). In attach_routing: `run_bd: &mut routing::BdRunner` — passing along. Note file_proposals with file_authorized=false returns Ok immediately; run_bd unused but we must still pass. Passing `run_bd: &mut BdRunner` generic lifetime fine.\\n\\nMain.rs:\\n\\n```rust\\nReport {\\n    path: String,\\n    /// Run the fault-routing step after aggregation (dry-run by\\n    /// default): attach the additive `routed` summary to the dashboard\\n    /// and write proposed-tickets.jsonl next to the report.\\n    #[arg(long)]\\n    route: bool,\\n    /// File the proposed tickets in their mapped repositories\\n    /// (explicit cross-repo write authorization). Requires --route.\\n    #[arg(long)]\\n    file: bool,\\n},\\n```\\n\\nrun_report:\\n\\n```rust\\nfn run_report(path: &str, route: bool, file: bool) -> Result<ExitCode, String> {\\n    evallerina::report::validate_route_flags(route, file)?;\\n    ... rows parse (existing) ...\\n    let configured: Vec<String> = ordered_ids()...;\\n    let mut dashboard = aggregate_rows(...)?;\\n    if route {\\n        evallerina::report::run_routing_step(\\n            &mut dashboard, &rows, &configured, std::path::Path::new(path), file, &mut evallerina::routing::run_bd,\\n        )?;\\n    }\\n    println!(...);\\n    Ok(ExitCode::SUCCESS)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nrun_routing_step in report.rs:\\n\\n```rust\\npub fn run_routing_step(\\n    dashboard: &mut serde_json::Value,\\n    rows: &[serde_json::Value],\\n    configured_models: &[String],\\n    report_path: &std::path::Path,\\n    file_authorized: bool,\\n    run_bd: &mut routing::BdRunner,\\n) -> Result<Vec<routing::ProposalRow>, String> {\\n    let content = std::fs::read_to_string(\\\"routing.toml\\\")\\n        .map_err(|e| format!(\\\"cannot read routing.toml: {e}\\\"))?;\\n    let repo_map = routing::parse_routing_toml(&content)?;\\n    let proposals = attach_routing(dashboard, rows, configured_models, &repo_map, file_authorized, run_bd)?;\\n    let jsonl = routing::to_jsonl(&proposals);\\n    let out_path = report_path.parent().unwrap_or(std::path::Path::new(\\\".\\\")).join(\\\"proposed-tickets.jsonl\\\");\\n    std::fs::write(&out_path, jsonl).map_err(|e| format!(\\\"cannot write {}: {e}\\\", out_path.display()))?;\\n    Ok(proposals)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: reading \\\"routing.toml\\\" from cwd. In bin tests, sandbox cwd must contain routing.toml (copy from manifest dir). In real usage (CI/local) cwd = repo root. Acceptable; document.\\n\\nHmm wait — `routing::run_bd` is a fn item; `&mut evallerina::routing::run_bd` coerces to &mut dyn FnMut? BdRunner = dyn FnMut(&Path, &[&str]) -> BdResult. `run_bd` fn signature matches. `&mut run_bd` — need a local binding: `let mut runner = evallerina::routing::run_bd; ... &mut runner` to coerce. Function item coerces to fn pointer then to dyn. `&mut evallerina::routing::run_bd` might coerce directly via unsized coercion? For a function item, `&mut F` → `&mut dyn FnMut` requires the function item to coerce to the trait object; unsized coercion from fn item reference... Standard pattern: define `let mut runner: routing::BdRunner = routing::run_bd;` hmm that's a binding of dyn type. Actually `fn file_proposals(..., run_bd: &mut BdRunner)`. Passing `&mut routing::run_bd` — the expression `routing::run_bd` is a fn item value; taking `&mut` gives `&mut FnDef`; coercion `&mut FnDef -> &mut dyn FnMut` works (function items implement FnMut). I believe `&mut run_bd` where run_bd is a path to a fn works since &mut fn item unsizes. Yes, unsized coercions apply to references of function items (they implement the Fn traits). To be safe, bind to a local va