{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- ah_check: Validate espectacular spec-test correspondence (ah check)\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Use ah_check after changing specs, contract TOMLs, or tests; use run_tests when executed verification is needed.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /var/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/Cellar/pi-coding-agent/0.87.1/libexec/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /var/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/Cellar/pi-coding-agent/0.87.1/libexec/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /var/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/Cellar/pi-coding-agent/0.87.1/libexec/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/var/home/sasha/AGENTS.md\">\n<!-- WAI:START --># Workflow Tools\n\nThis project uses **wai** to track the *why* behind decisions — research,\nreasoning, and design choices that shaped the code. Run `wai status` first\nto orient yourself.\n\nDetected workflow tools:\n- **wai** — research, reasoning, and design decisions\n- **beads** — issue tracking (tasks, bugs, dependencies). CLI command: **`bd`** (not `beads`)\n\n> **CRITICAL**: Apply TDD and Tidy First throughout — not just when writing code:\n> - **Planning/task creation**: each ticket should map to a red→green→refactor cycle; refactoring tasks must be separate tickets from feature tasks.\n> - **Design**: define the test shape (inputs/outputs) before designing the implementation.\n> - **Implementation**: write the failing test first, then make it pass, then tidy in a separate commit.\n\n> **When beginning research or creating a ticket**: run `wai search \"<topic>\"` to check for existing patterns before writing new content.\n\n## Quick Start\n\n1. `wai sync` — ensure agent tools are projected\n2. `wai status` — see active projects, phase, and suggestions\n3. `bd ready` — find available work items\n\nWhen context reaches ~40%: stop and tell the user — responses degrade past\nthis point. Recommend `wai close` then `/clear` to resume cleanly.\nDo NOT skip `wai close` — it enables resume detection.\n\n## Autonomous Work Policy\n\nProceed without routine confirmation when the next step is clear.\nDo not ask to continue, fix, or commit — just do it.\n\n**Stop and ask** only when:\n- Conflicting requirements or ambiguous intent\n- Destructive actions (data loss, force-push, drop table)\n- Credentials, secrets, or external services not yet authorized\n- Unresolved test failures after two attempts\n- Push, deploy, or release — always get explicit authorization\n- Context approaching 40% — recommend `wai close` then `/clear`\n\n## Detailed Instructions\n\nFull workflow reference — session lifecycle, capturing work, command cheat\nsheets, cross-tool sync, and PARA structure — lives in **`.wai/AGENTS.md`**.\nRead it at the start of your first session or when you need detailed guidance.\n\nKeep this managed block so `wai init` can refresh the instructions.\n\n<!-- WAI:END -->\n\n# Global Agent Instructions\n\n- Prefer `just` commands over custom shell scripts — check `just --global-justfile --list` first\n- All notes in `.org` format (org-mode syntax)\n- Dotfiles managed with chezmoi — source at `~/para/areas/dev/gh/ak/dotfiles`\n- **beads tracked-export invariant**: in every repo, `.beads/issues.jsonl` must be the source of truth (`no-db: true` in `.beads/config.yaml`), git-tracked, and fresh. After any `bd` issue change: `bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl && git add -f .beads/issues.jsonl && git commit` (the global `~/.gitignore` excludes `.beads/` — a plain `git add` silently no-ops). `ddl doctor` flags violations (`ddl.repo.beads-export`); `ddl init --gates` re-wires it idempotently.\n\n## Details\n\nFull workflows and references in `~/.config/agents/`:\n\n| File | Contents |\n|------|----------|\n| [`~/.config/agents/chezmoi.md`](~/.config/agents/chezmoi.md) | Dotfiles management, tracked files, chezmoi commands |\n| [`~/.config/agents/note-taking.md`](~/.config/agents/note-taking.md) | `just` note commands, repo locations, agent-friendly sync |\n| [`~/.config/agents/stream-journaling.md`](~/.config/agents/stream-journaling.md) | Stream journaling methodology and role |\n| [`~/.config/agents/canticos.md`](~/.config/agents/canticos.md) | Personal CLI tools from `~/para/areas/dev/gh/charly/canticos` (terapia, ci-usage, …) |\n\n## Personal CLI tools (canticos)\n\n- **terapia** — typed query CLI (nushell) over Pi agent sessions: `terapia list|tools|errors|stats|raw`. Use it INSTEAD of writing bespoke python/heredoc parsers over `~/.pi/agent/sessions`; output is JSON, incremental mtime-keyed cache in `~/.cache/terapia`.\n  - Useful for post-mortems: `terapia errors --all --group-by class`, `terapia tools <session-id-prefix> --errors-only`.\n  - Tests: `python3 ~/para/areas/dev/gh/charly/canticos/tests/test_terapia.py`\n\n## Context discipline\n\n- Each distinct task gets its own session — do not chain unrelated work in one session\n- Summarize tool outputs longer than 50 lines before continuing; never dump full output into context\n- When reading files >200 lines, read only the relevant section\n- If a tool call fails 2× in a row, stop and report rather than retry\n- Research subagents return a ≤500-token summary, not raw findings, into the parent context\n\n<!-- WAI:REFLECT:REF:START -->\n## Accumulated Project Patterns\n\nProject-specific conventions, gotchas, and architecture notes live in\n`.wai/resources/reflections/`. Run `wai search \"<topic>\"` to retrieve relevant\ncontext before starting research or creating tickets.\n\n> **Before research or ticket creation**: always run `wai search \"<topic>\"` to\n> check for known patterns. Do not rediscover what is already documented.\n<!-- WAI:REFLECT:REF:END -->\n\n</project_instructions>\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/var/home/sasha/para/areas/dev/gh/charly/AGENTS.md\">\n# Agent Instructions\n\nThis project uses **bd** (beads) for issue tracking. Run `bd prime` for full workflow context.\n\n> **Architecture in one line:** issues live in `.beads/issues.jsonl` (JSONL, committed to git — the source of truth). bd runs **embedded** (in-process Dolt, `no-db: true`): there is no dolt remote and no `bd dolt push/pull` sync. Git itself is the sync mechanism.\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n```bash\nbd ready              # Find available work\nbd show <id>          # View issue details\nbd update <id> --claim  # Claim work atomically\nbd close <id>         # Complete work\n```\n\n## Non-Interactive Shell Commands\n\n**ALWAYS use non-interactive flags** with file operations to avoid hanging on confirmation prompts.\n\nShell commands like `cp`, `mv`, and `rm` may be aliased to include `-i` (interactive) mode on some systems, causing the agent to hang indefinitely waiting for y/n input.\n\n**Use these forms instead:**\n```bash\n# Force overwrite without prompting\ncp -f source dest           # NOT: cp source dest\nmv -f source dest           # NOT: mv source dest\nrm -f file                  # NOT: rm file\n\n# For recursive operations\nrm -rf directory            # NOT: rm -r directory\ncp -rf source dest          # NOT: cp -r source dest\n```\n\n**Other commands that may prompt:**\n- `scp` - use `-o BatchMode=yes` for non-interactive\n- `ssh` - use `-o BatchMode=yes` to fail instead of prompting\n- `apt-get` - use `-y` flag\n- `brew` - use `HOMEBREW_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1` env var\n\n<!-- BEGIN BEADS INTEGRATION v:1 profile:minimal hash:7510c1e2 -->\n## Beads Issue Tracker\n\nThis project uses **bd (beads)** for issue tracking. Run `bd prime` to see full workflow context and commands.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n```bash\nbd ready              # Find available work\nbd show <id>          # View issue details\nbd update <id> --claim  # Claim work\nbd close <id>         # Complete work\n```\n\n### Rules\n\n- Use `bd` for ALL task tracking — do NOT use TodoWrite, TaskCreate, or markdown TODO lists\n- Run `bd prime` for detailed command reference and session close protocol\n- Use `bd remember` for persistent knowledge — do NOT use MEMORY.md files\n\n**Architecture in one line:** issues live in `.beads/issues.jsonl` (JSONL in git, source of truth); bd runs embedded with `no-db: true` — no dolt remote, no `bd dolt push/pull`. See https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/blob/main/docs/SYNC_CONCEPTS.md for anti-patterns.\n\n## Session Completion\n\n**When ending a work session**, you MUST complete ALL steps below. Work is NOT complete until `git push` succeeds.\n\n**MANDATORY WORKFLOW:**\n\n1. **File issues for remaining work** - Create issues for anything that needs follow-up\n2. **Run quality gates** (if code changed) - Tests, linters, builds\n3. **Update issue status** - Close finished work, update in-progress items\n4. **PUSH TO REMOTE** - This is MANDATORY:\n   ```bash\n   git pull --rebase\n   git push\n   git status  # MUST show \"up to date with origin\"\n   ```\n5. **Clean up** - Clear stashes, prune remote branches\n6. **Verify** - All changes committed AND pushed\n7. **Hand off** - Provide context for next session\n\n**CRITICAL RULES:**\n- Work is NOT complete until `git push` succeeds\n- NEVER stop before pushing - that leaves work stranded locally\n- NEVER say \"ready to push when you are\" - YOU must push\n- If push fails, resolve and retry until it succeeds\n<!-- END BEADS INTEGRATION -->\n\n</project_instructions>\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/var/home/sasha/para/areas/dev/gh/charly/evallerina/AGENTS.md\">\n<!-- OPENSPEC:START -->\n# OpenSpec Instructions\n\nThese instructions are for AI assistants working in this project.\n\nAlways open `@/openspec/AGENTS.md` when the request:\n- Mentions planning or proposals (words like proposal, spec, change, plan)\n- Introduces new capabilities, breaking changes, architecture shifts, or big performance/security work\n- Sounds ambiguous and you need the authoritative spec before coding\n\nUse `@/openspec/AGENTS.md` to learn:\n- How to create and apply change proposals\n- Spec format and conventions\n- Project structure and guidelines\n\nKeep this managed block so 'openspec update' can refresh the instructions.\n\n<!-- OPENSPEC:END -->\n\n<!-- WAI:START --># Workflow Tools\n\nThis project uses **wai** to track the *why* behind decisions — research,\nreasoning, and design choices that shaped the code. Run `wai status` first\nto orient yourself.\n\nDetected workflow tools:\n- **wai** — research, reasoning, and design decisions\n- **beads** — issue tracking (tasks, bugs, dependencies). CLI command: **`bd`** (not `beads`)\n- **openspec** — specifications and change proposals (see `openspec/AGENTS.md`)\n\n> **CRITICAL**: Apply TDD and Tidy First throughout — not just when writing code:\n> - **Planning/task creation**: each ticket should map to a red→green→refactor cycle; refactoring tasks must be separate tickets from feature tasks.\n> - **Design**: define the test shape (inputs/outputs) before designing the implementation.\n> - **Implementation**: write the failing test first, then make it pass, then tidy in a separate commit.\n\n> **When beginning research or creating a ticket**: run `wai search \"<topic>\"` to check for existing patterns before writing new content.\n\n## Quick Start\n\n1. `wai sync` — ensure agent tools are projected\n2. `wai status` — see active projects, phase, and suggestions\n3. `bd ready` — find available work items\n\nWhen context reaches ~40%: stop and tell the user — responses degrade past\nthis point. Recommend `wai close` then `/clear` to resume cleanly.\nDo NOT skip `wai close` — it enables resume detection.\n\n## Autonomous Work Policy\n\nProceed without routine confirmation when the next step is clear.\nDo not ask to continue, fix, or commit — just do it.\n\n**Stop and ask** only when:\n- Conflicting requirements or ambiguous intent\n- Destructive actions (data loss, force-push, drop table)\n- Credentials, secrets, or external services not yet authorized\n- Unresolved test failures after two attempts\n- Push, deploy, or release — always get explicit authorization\n- Context approaching 40% — recommend `wai close` then `/clear`\n\n## Detailed Instructions\n\nFull workflow reference — session lifecycle, capturing work, command cheat\nsheets, cross-tool sync, and PARA structure — lives in **`.wai/AGENTS.md`**.\nRead it at the start of your first session or when you need detailed guidance.\n\nKeep this managed block so `wai init` can refresh the instructions.\n\n\n<!-- provenance: generator=wai version=0.12.1 source=WAI sha=3e02f7c4 -->\n<!-- WAI:END -->\n<!-- WAI:REFLECT:REF:START -->\n## Accumulated Project Patterns\n\nProject-specific conventions, gotchas, and architecture notes live in\n`.wai/resources/reflections/`. Run `wai search \"<topic>\"` to retrieve relevant\ncontext before starting research or creating tickets.\n\n> **Before research or ticket creation**: always run `wai search \"<topic>\"` to\n> check for known patterns. Do not rediscover what is already documented.\n\n\n<!-- provenance: generator=wai version=0.12.1 source=WAI:REFLECT:REF sha=73879972 -->\n<!-- WAI:REFLECT:REF:END -->\n\n<!-- BEGIN BEADS INTEGRATION v:1 profile:minimal hash:7510c1e2 -->\n## Beads Issue Tracker\n\nThis project uses **bd (beads)** for issue tracking. Run `bd prime` to see full workflow context and commands.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\n```bash\nbd ready              # Find available work\nbd show <id>          # View issue details\nbd update <id> --claim  # Claim work\nbd close <id>         # Complete work\n```\n\n### Rules\n\n- Use `bd` for ALL task tracking — do NOT use TodoWrite, TaskCreate, or markdown TODO lists\n- Run `bd prime` for detailed command reference and session close protocol\n- Use `bd remember` for persistent knowledge — do NOT use MEMORY.md files\n\n**Architecture in one line:** issues live in a local Dolt DB; sync uses `refs/dolt/data` on your git remote; `.beads/issues.jsonl` is a passive export. See https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/blob/main/docs/SYNC_CONCEPTS.md for details and anti-patterns.\n\n## Session Completion\n\n**When ending a work session**, you MUST complete ALL steps below. Work is NOT complete until `git push` succeeds.\n\n**MANDATORY WORKFLOW:**\n\n1. **File issues for remaining work** - Create issues for anything that needs follow-up\n2. **Run quality gates** (if code changed) - Tests, linters, builds\n3. **Update issue status** - Close finished work, update in-progress items\n4. **PUSH TO REMOTE** - This is MANDATORY:\n   ```bash\n   git pull --rebase\n   git push\n   git status  # MUST show \"up to date with origin\"\n   ```\n5. **Clean up** - Clear stashes, prune remote branches\n6. **Verify** - All changes committed AND pushed\n7. **Hand off** - Provide context for next session\n\n**CRITICAL RULES:**\n- Work is NOT complete until `git push` succeeds\n- NEVER stop before pushing - that leaves work stranded locally\n- NEVER say \"ready to push when you are\" - YOU must push\n- If push fails, resolve and retry until it succeeds\n<!-- END BEADS INTEGRATION -->\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>review</name>\n    <description>Multi-pass reviews of code, plans, specs, or docs: general code review, Rule-of-5 iterative refinement, parallel agent review waves, multi-agent coordination, adversarial red-team pass, guided teaching review. Trigger on review requests.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/skills/review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>session</name>\n    <description>Session lifecycle: end-of-session wrap-up and commit (close), quick stash (next), park before context switch, renew to resume prior work, create or resume handoff documents. Trigger for closing, wrapping up, parking, resuming, or handoffs.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/skills/session/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>typesafe-ai</name>\n    <description>Build AI-powered software with TypeSafe: small units of AI intelligence you can use like programming primitives. Its System One models, including Jev, turn natural language and application state into typed judgments and probabilities that code can combine. Use when a feature needs programmable common sense, when brainstorming what AI could make possible in an app, or when an LLM prompt-and-parse step could become a structured decision. Applications include routing, ranking, extraction, verification, and interactive experiences; these are starting points, not the limits. Read live docs and cookbooks to find useful patterns and discover new combinations.\n</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/skills/typesafe-ai/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>abstraction-miner</name>\n    <description>Scan codebase for semantic duplication and propose reusable abstractions — advisory only, no code changes. Use when hunting duplicated code to extract abstractions.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/abstraction-miner/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>adversarial-stakeholder-evaluation</name>\n    <description>Three-tier adversarial evaluation of proposals: stakeholder council, anti-persona stress testing, pre-mortem with contradiction analysis. Use when stress-testing a proposal against hostile stakeholders.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/adversarial-stakeholder-evaluation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>agent-value-alignment</name>\n    <description>Audit or establish the closed loop anchoring an agent&apos;s behavior to its intended value across four layers (Value, Governance, Runtime Prompt, Infrastructure); detects goal and value drift early. Use when auditing value alignment or investigating goal drift.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/agent-value-alignment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>anti-slop-prose</name>\n    <description>Tag AI-generated prose for overused vocabulary, formulaic constructions, and vague claims — advisory-only, no rewrites. Use when checking AI-written prose for slop.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/anti-slop-prose/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>archive</name>\n    <description>Move a completed project or inactive area to the archive. Trigger when the user says /archive &lt;name&gt;, &apos;close project &lt;name&gt;&apos;, or &apos;archive &lt;name&gt;&apos;.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/archive/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>bias-audit</name>\n    <description>Detect, prevent, and mitigate systemic, statistical, and cognitive biases using multidisciplinary frameworks. Use when auditing a decision or analysis for bias.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/bias-audit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>capture</name>\n    <description>Add a task, note, or follow-up to the work journal inbox. Trigger when the user says /capture, &apos;note that&apos;, &apos;add to inbox&apos;, &apos;remember to&apos;, or mentions a task to track.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/capture/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>close</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the end-of-session wrap-up mode of the session skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:close; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/close/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>codebase-cartography</name>\n    <description>Map a codebase&apos;s structure as text-based architecture diagrams at macro, meso, and micro zoom levels, with optional structural-health triage. Use when mapping codebase architecture as diagrams.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/codebase-cartography/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>commit</name>\n    <description>Create well-structured, atomic git commits with clear intent. Use when the user asks to commit changes.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/commit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>context-guardian</name>\n    <description>System prompt that enforces reuse of existing abstractions and prevents architectural drift during development. Use when enforcing architecture reuse during development.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/context-guardian/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>create-handoff</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the handoff document mode of the session skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:create-handoff; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/create-handoff/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>create-issues</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the issue creation mode of the issues skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:create-issues; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/create-issues/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>debug</name>\n    <description>7-step diagnostic workflow for debugging issues. Use when interactively debugging a live failure.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/debug/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>describe-pr</name>\n    <description>Generate clear PR descriptions from code changes. Use when opening or updating a pull request.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/describe-pr/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>design-practice</name>\n    <description>Rich Hickey-inspired design methodology workflow. Use when a design must precede implementation.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/design-practice/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>design-review</name>\n    <description>Review problem statements and decision matrices. Use only when reviewing problem statements or decision matrices.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/design-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>diagnostician</name>\n    <description>Advisory-only diagnostics, no code changes: modularity, rigidity, mutability, invalid states, composability, error handling, Julia performance, UX/DX evaluation, spec evaluation, testability, verification, root-cause analysis. Use when diagnosing design flaws, advisory-only.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/diagnostician/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>doc-link-verifier</name>\n    <description>Audit docs for broken links and contextual correctness: HTTP status, relative paths, anchors, and link-text/target mismatches. Use when auditing documentation links and cross-references.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/doc-link-verifier/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>documentation</name>\n    <description>Documentation suites: audit or plan docs, write with Diátaxis/Info Mapping/EPPO, review for scannability and AI-readiness. Trigger for doc planning, writing, or review. Broken links are doc-link-verifier, not this.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/documentation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>edge-case-discovery</name>\n    <description>Discover edge cases, failure modes, and spec gaps before implementation using the six-boundary framework (Human, Business, Mathematical, Architectural, Failure, Formal). Use before implementation to surface edge cases.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/edge-case-discovery/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>file-headers</name>\n    <description>Enforce a Purpose/Responsibilities/Rationale header on every source file so agents learn intent without reverse-engineering. Use when creating or reviewing source files.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/file-headers/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>formal-verification-evaluator</name>\n    <description>Evaluate specs for formal-verification readiness: paradigm, safety/liveness properties, invalidity risk, toolchain choice, scoping. Use when deciding whether to formally verify a spec.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/formal-verification-evaluator/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>grill-me</name>\n    <description>Interview the user relentlessly about a plan or design until shared understanding is reached. Use when the user asks to be grilled.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/grill-me/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>issue-review</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the issue review mode of the issues skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:issue-review; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/issue-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>issues</name>\n    <description>Issue tracking: create trackable issues from an implementation plan, or review existing issues for completeness, dependencies, and executability. Trigger for breaking work into issues or checking issue quality.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/issues/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>jlog</name>\n    <description>Open or create today&apos;s daily log entry and append notes. Trigger when the user says /jlog, &apos;log this&apos;, &apos;add to today&apos;s log&apos;, or wants to record a decision or session note.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/jlog/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>migrate</name>\n    <description>Bullet journal migration ritual. Process inbox, review waiting tasks, clean up stale items. Trigger when the user says /migrate, &apos;process inbox&apos;, &apos;clean up tasks&apos;, or &apos;review tasks&apos;.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/migrate/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>narrative-article</name>\n    <description>Create engaging technical articles with narrative structure. Use when writing a story-driven technical article.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/narrative-article/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>next</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the quick stash mode of the session skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:next; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/next/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>openspec-apply</name>\n    <description>Implements an approved OpenSpec change and keeps tasks in sync. Use when applying or implementing an openspec proposal.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/openspec-apply/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>openspec-archive</name>\n    <description>Archives a deployed OpenSpec change and updates specs. Use when archiving a completed openspec change.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/openspec-archive/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>openspec-proposal</name>\n    <description>Scaffolds a new OpenSpec change and validates strictly. Use when creating a new openspec proposal or change.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/openspec-proposal/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optionality-review</name>\n    <description>Evaluate design decisions for flexibility and reversibility. Use when weighing reversible versus irreversible design choices.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/optionality-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>park</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the context-switch stash mode of the session skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:park; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/park/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>planning</name>\n    <description>Implementation planning: create a phased TDD-oriented plan, implement it phase by phase, review a plan for gaps and risks, or iterate it from feedback. Trigger for planning or executing multi-phase work.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/planning/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>pre-mortem</name>\n    <description>Anticipate failures before they happen. Use before a plan is approved or executed.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/pre-mortem/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>project-status-report</name>\n    <description>Create audience-specific project status reports grounded in evidence, variance, blockers, decisions, and agentic AI provenance when applicable. Use when reporting project status to stakeholders.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/project-status-report/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>prompt-meta</name>\n    <description>Prompt engineering on this repo: extract a reusable prompt from a successful interaction, distill it to token-efficient form, verify the distilled version preserved essential instructions. Trigger for capture/distill/verify prompt work.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/prompt-meta/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>pwa-accessibility-review</name>\n    <description>4-domain heuristic review of PWAs targeting non-technical merchants on entry-level devices with volatile connectivity. Use when reviewing PWAs for non-technical low-end users.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/pwa-accessibility-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>renew</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the resume prior work mode of the session skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:renew; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/renew/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>research-codebase</name>\n    <description>Structured investigation of unfamiliar codebases. Use when onboarding to an unfamiliar codebase.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/research-codebase/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>research-review</name>\n    <description>Iterative review of research documents using Rule of 5. Use only when iteratively reviewing research documents.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/research-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>resonant-refactor</name>\n    <description>Safe, human-approved refactoring workflow with impact analysis, atomic execution, and automatic rollback. Use when refactoring with human approval gates.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/resonant-refactor/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>rule-of-5-universal</name>\n    <description>Compiled alias of the Rule-of-5 universal review mode of the review skill. Hidden from model invocation; invocable via /skill:rule-of-5-universal; serves the legacy site URL with full compiled content.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/rule-of-5-universal/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>specification-review</name>\n    <description>Iterative review of technical specifications for autonomy, precision, and AI-readiness. Use only when reviewing a written spec document for quality.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/specification-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>systematic-housekeeping</name>\n    <description>Comprehensive repository maintenance across Code/CI, Documentation, Prompts/Skills, and Context/Artifacts using 5S principles. Use for periodic whole-repository maintenance passes.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/systematic-housekeeping/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tdd</name>\n    <description>Test-driven development workflow. Use when writing tests before implementation.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-abstraction-miner</name>\n    <description>Detect Lazy Test clusters in test suites and propose parameterized tests and property-based test escalations — advisory only, no code changes. Use when test suites show copy-paste duplication.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/test-abstraction-miner/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-friction</name>\n    <description>Diagnose production code design flaws by reading test pain signals — advisory only, no code changes. Use when tests are painful to write or maintain.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/test-friction/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ui-align</name>\n    <description>High-alignment UI specification workflow using interactive grilling and methodology verification. Use when specifying UI through interactive grilling.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/ui-align/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>whisper</name>\n    <description>Deterministic knowledge workspace: session loop (recall all / append), scope routing, distill, bundle. Delegates to the turu CLI when installed; manual fallbacks otherwise.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/whisper/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>wrap-up</name>\n    <description>End-of-day review across all projects. Synthesizes work-queue entries into the log, flags undocumented work. Trigger when the user says /wrap-up, &apos;end of day&apos;, &apos;day review&apos;, or &apos;daily close&apos;.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.agents/skills/wrap-up/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>fck</name>\n    <description>Finish the sentence the user didn&apos;t write. Trigger when the user&apos;s message is primarily a swear — &quot;fuck&quot;, &quot;shit&quot;, &quot;oh shit&quot;, &quot;oh fuck&quot;, &quot;wtf&quot;, or elongated variants like &quot;fuuuuuk&quot; — instead of a description of a problem. Also for the explicit /fuck, /shit, /oh-shit, /oh-fuck, /fuuuuuk commands. The swear carries no content; the content is in what just happened on screen. Go look — last command, last edit, last error — and hand back a concrete diagnosis or the specific missing sentence, instead of asking &quot;what&apos;s wrong?&quot;, mirroring emotion, or lecturing about message quality. Extra repeated letters = more urgency (&quot;fuuuuuk&quot; vs &quot;fuck&quot;).</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/git/github.com/charly-vibes/fck/skills/fck/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>for-fucks-sake</name>\n    <description>Interview fallback when there is genuinely nothing in context to explain the user&apos;s frustration — no error, no recent edit, no obvious cause for a &quot;fuck&quot;/&quot;shit&quot;/&quot;wtf&quot;. Instead of guessing or asking an open-ended &quot;what&apos;s wrong?&quot;, ask exactly one question at a time, each with a best-guess answer attached so the user can confirm in one word. Also invoked directly as /for-fucks-sake.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/git/github.com/charly-vibes/fck/skills/for-fucks-sake/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>no-shit</name>\n    <description>Verification pass before calling any fix &quot;done&quot; — especially incident-tier fixes (/oh-shit, /oh-fuck, intensity 4-5). Check real output instead of trusting that the change was made, scan once for side effects, then state the verdict plainly. Use after applying any fix whose failure would matter, or when invoked directly as /no-shit. Replaces the one-shot &quot;fixed it&quot; claim with actual confirmation, stopping the moment it&apos;s genuinely confirmed.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/git/github.com/charly-vibes/fck/skills/no-shit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>would-you-please-fucking-stop</name>\n    <description>Circuit-breaker for when the agent itself is the problem — looping on the same failed fix, hedging, restating the same answer without new information, or producing repetition instead of progress. Named after Ursula K. Le Guin&apos;s essay &quot;Would You Please Fucking Stop?&quot;. Use when frustration is aimed at how the conversation is going rather than at the task. Forces a reset to actual current state and exactly one concrete next step. Also invoked directly as /would-you-please-fucking-stop.</description>\n    <location>/var/home/sasha/.pi/agent/git/github.com/charly-vibes/fck/skills/would-you-please-fucking-stop/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/var/home/sasha/para/areas/dev/gh/charly/evallerina\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1792429797690,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"ah_check","description":"Validate spec-test correspondence for the espectacular setup in the project: checks every deployed spec scenario against its contract TOML and, with run_tests, executes the declared contract tests. Prefer this over running `ah check` via bash — it returns the structured JSON envelope. Exit code 1 means findings exist.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"changes":{"type":"string","description":"In-flight openspec change name to validate with (change overlay)"},"project":{"type":"string","description":"Project root directory (defaults to the session working directory)"},"run_tests":{"type":"boolean","description":"Also execute declared contract tests (slower; default false)"}}}},{"name":"ah_doctor","description":"Diagnose the espectacular setup in the project: config, version drift, specs/changes dirs, managed blocks, hooks, and framework recommendations. Read-only.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"project":{"type":"string","description":"Project root directory (defaults to the session working directory)"}}}},{"name":"ah_signals","description":"Emit dont drift signals from the espectacular setup in the project. Read-only.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"project":{"type":"string","description":"Project root directory (defaults to the session working directory)"}}}},{"name":"ah_explain","description":"Playbook guidance for an espectacular finding kind, suggested action, or topic (e.g. no-tests-declared, contract-stale, workflow, scenario-scoped-tests). Returns the full guidance text.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["topic"],"properties":{"topic":{"type":"string","description":"Finding kind, suggested action, or topic slug"},"project":{"type":"string","description":"Project root directory (defaults to the session working directory)"}}}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are implementing ticket evallerina-o3j in a FRESH session (no prior history). Read the brief at .wai/projects/evallerina/briefs/evallerina-o3j.md and follow it exactly: red→green steps 1-5 wiring --route/--file flags into src/main.rs, additive routed summary into src/report.rs, tests in tests/report_aggregation.rs, GHA artifact upload in .github/workflows/evals-rotation.yml. Read src/routing.rs first — it provides propose() and the filing path; do not modify it except imports/use. Anti-goals: routed field additive (report_version stays 1), no aggregate score, CI never passes --file. Gate: cargo test --test report_aggregation green per step; cargo test + just tier0 green at end. Commit atomically per green step referencing evallerina-o3j. Do NOT push, close tickets, or edit .beads/. Finish with a report: commits, tests added, tier0 result."}],"timestamp":1792429797688},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e17f24f506fe4d39a5bcb686","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":200,"offset":1,"path":".wai/projects/evallerina/briefs/evallerina-o3j.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d230e2263e0a49e68a78c3ee","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls src tests .github/workflows 2>/dev/null","timeout":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":11050,"output":66,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":11116,"cost":{"input":0.0016575,"output":0.000032999999999999996,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0016905}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792429797700,"responseId":"gen-1792429797-odiXfDqvSV33CinRovjE","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_e17f24f506fe4d39a5bcb686","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Brief: evallerina-o3j — CLI --route flag + routed field + GHA artifact\n\nTicket: evallerina-o3j (P2, afk, complexity:m) — Phase 3 of openspec\n`add-fault-ticket-routing`, tasks 3.1–3.2. Builds on src/routing.rs\n(proposals, tasks 1.x) and its filing path (tasks 2.x, closed). Read\nsrc/routing.rs, src/report.rs (aggregate_rows + scenario_channel),\nsrc/main.rs, tests/report_aggregation.rs first.\n\n## Why\nWiring is cross-cutting (CLI + report schema + CI) but each change is\nmechanical; a subagent with the exact surface list avoids the lead\nre-deriving the report contract.\n\n## Desired outcome\n`evallerina report --route [--file]` runs the routing step and records the\nadditive `routed` summary; the tier-2 GHA rotation uploads the dry-run\nartifact; CI never passes `--file`.\n\n## Exact files\n- `src/main.rs` — add `--route` and `--file` flags to the report subcommand\n  (`--file` requires `--route`; error otherwise)\n- `src/report.rs` — call routing::propose after aggregation when `--route`;\n  attach `routed` summary {filed, existing, dry_run, skipped} to the report\n  JSON; write proposed-tickets.jsonl next to the report when routing ran\n- `tests/report_aggregation.rs` — extend: routed field present after\n  --route dry-run; report_version still 1; --file without --route is an\n  error; absent rows never routed\n- `.github/workflows/evals-rotation.yml` — upload proposed-tickets.jsonl as\n  workflow artifact on scheduled runs; no `--file` anywhere\n\n## Red/green sequence\n1. RED: --file without --route → loud error. GREEN.\n2. RED: report --route (dry-run) on a fabricated rows file → `routed`\n   summary present with dry_run count > 0, report_version 1 unchanged,\n   proposed-tickets.jsonl written. GREEN.\n3. RED: report without --route → no `routed` field, no jsonl. GREEN.\n4. RED: routing failures (unmapped tool) → skipped counted, report still\n   succeeds. GREEN.\n5. Edit evals-rotation.yml: add artifact upload step for\n   proposed-tickets.jsonl. Grep-assert: no \"--file\" in the workflow.\n\n## Commands\n- `cargo test --test report_aggregation` per step; `cargo test` + `just\n  tier0` green at end.\n\n## Anti-goals\n- routed field is additive; report_version stays 1\n- no aggregate score field\n- CI/scheduled workflows must never pass --file\n- do not modify src/routing.rs except import/use\n\n## Follow-up threshold\nIf the report JSON contract resists the additive field (>1h), STOP and\nrecord findings.\n\n## Spawn model\nModel: sonnet or default. Context ceiling: ≤ 50% at spawn.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792429806178},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_d230e2263e0a49e68a78c3ee","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":".github/workflows:\nevals-ci.yml\nevals-rotation.yml\n\nsrc:\nablation.rs\nenvelope.rs\nlib.rs\nlive.rs\nmain.rs\nrecorded.rs\nregistry.rs\nreport.rs\nrouting.rs\nscenario.rs\n\ntests:\nablation.rs\nevals_replay.rs\nlive_protocol.rs\nreport_aggregation.rs\nrouting.rs\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792429806179},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_df9651c9e47348b8afd4b6f4","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":150,"offset":1,"path":"src/routing.rs"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_870b61bcd0fb4225abeee846","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":150,"offset":1,"path":"src/report.rs"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":858,"output":49,"cacheRead":11008,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":11915,"cost":{"input":0.00012869999999999998,"output":0.0000245,"cacheRead":0.00033024,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00048343999999999997}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792429806182,"responseId":"gen-1792429806-26RWAAYK1QVeg2QQ3nrR","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_df9651c9e47348b8afd4b6f4","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"//! Purpose: Dry-run fault routing — proposals for tool-fault tickets in\n//! the faulting tool's repository (openspec add-fault-ticket-routing).\n//! Responsibilities: filter tier-2 report rows to tool faults (failing\n//! checks without an `ERR_*` code), tally DISTINCT models per fault key\n//! (scenario, fault-class, tool) using the same threshold semantics as\n//! report.rs (min(3, |configured model registry|)), key the target\n//! repository on the scenario definition's recorded tool (never the\n//! report row's tool field, which is hardcoded for live trials), and\n//! emit proposal rows with the fingerprint token\n//! `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>` and the full machine-checkable\n//! schema. Phase 1 is dry-run only: every emitted decision is `dry_run`\n//! (mapped tool) or `skipped`/`unmapped_tool` — no `bd` invocations\n//! happen in the dry-run path. Phase 2 (evallerina-2bz) adds the filing\n//! path behind explicit authorization: fingerprint-token dedup in the\n//! target repo (`--status open` always pinned — closed issues must not\n//! block re-filing a regressed fault), then `bd create` in the target\n//! repo cwd with exit-code + id capture, then the tracked-export\n//! invariant (`bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl`). Every bd invocation\n//! is routed through `run_bd` (cwd-scoped subprocess) and is injectable\n//! so tests can spy on calls; `bd` failure is `skipped`/`bd_failure`,\n//! never fatal — filing is an optimization over the report.\n//! Rationale: the fault taxonomy routes tool faults to tickets in the\n//! tool's repo, but the value that made evallerina useful (evallerina-2rr,\n//! -00n, -54f) all required hand-filing from dashboard findings. Routing\n//! makes that mechanical, with a dry-run default so cross-repo writes\n//! stay behind an explicit local opt-in, and loud errors on unknown\n//! scenarios (mirroring report.rs: never a silent bucket).\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\n/// The cross-model routing threshold — identical semantics to\n/// report.rs: a fault key observed in trial rows from at least this\n/// many distinct models (3, or the full configured registry when\n/// smaller) is routed.\nconst ROUTING_THRESHOLD: usize = 3;\n\n/// One dry-run proposal row (the proposed-tickets.jsonl schema, design\n/// §6): machine-checkable so the CI artifact is reviewable without\n/// reading code.\n#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\npub struct ProposalRow {\n    /// The scenario definition's recorded tool — the repo key.\n    pub tool: String,\n    /// Mapped repository path (null until/unless the tool is mapped).\n    pub repo: Option<String>,\n    pub scenario: String,\n    pub fault_class: String,\n    /// Fingerprint token: `evallerina:<scenario>:<fault-class>`.\n    pub token: String,\n    /// `dry_run` (mapped, not filed) or `skipped` (unmapped tool).\n    pub decision: String,\n    /// Null unless skipped (`unmapped_tool`).\n    pub reason: Option<String>,\n    /// Null in dry-run; set only when a ticket is filed (Phase 2).\n    pub bd_issue_id: Option<String>,\n    /// Provenance of the contributing trial rows (deduped by model):\n    /// report row scenario/model/tool values.\n    pub row_provenance: Vec<ProvenanceRow>,\n}\n\n/// One contributing trial row's provenance.\n#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\npub struct ProvenanceRow {\n    pub scenario: String,\n    pub model: String,\n    /// The report row's tool value — provenance only, never the repo key.\n    pub tool: String,\n}\n\nimpl ProposalRow {\n    /// Serialize to the proposed-tickets.jsonl JSON object.\n    pub fn to_json(&self) -> serde_json::Value {\n        serde_json::json!({\n            \"tool\": self.tool,\n            \"repo\": self.repo,\n            \"scenario\": self.scenario,\n            \"fault_class\": self.fault_class,\n            \"token\": self.token,\n            \"decision\": self.decision,\n            \"reason\": self.reason,\n            \"bd_issue_id\": self.bd_issue_id,\n            \"row_provenance\": self.row_provenance.iter()\n                .map(|p| serde_json::json!({\n                    \"scenario\": p.scenario,\n                    \"model\": p.model,\n                    \"tool\": p.tool,\n                }))\n                .collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n        })\n    }\n}\n\n/// Propose dry-run tickets from tier-2 report rows.\n///\n/// `rows` are parsed report rows (same inputs as report.rs: tier-2\n/// trial events, tier-1 rows without model attribution, absent records).\n/// `configured_models` is the model registry the rotation was configured\n/// with — it defines the routing threshold (min(3, registry size));\n/// empty is a loud error, no routing threshold exists. `scenario_tool_of`\n/// resolves a scenario to the tool its *definition* recorded (proposals\n/// key the repo on this, never on the row's tool field); an unknown\n/// scenario is a loud error. `repo_map` maps tool name → repo path (the\n/// parsed routing.toml); a mapped-but-unregistered tool surfaces as\n/// `skipped`/`unmapped_tool`, never a silent drop.\npub fn propose(\n    rows: &[serde_json::Value],\n    configured_models: &[String],\n    scenario_tool_of: &dyn Fn(&str) -> Option<String>,\n    repo_map: &BTreeMap<String, String>,\n) -> Result<Vec<ProposalRow>, String> {\n    if configured_models.is_empty() {\n        return Err(\"configured model registry is empty — no routing threshold exists\".into());\n    }\n    let routing_threshold = configured_models.len().min(ROUTING_THRESHOLD);\n\n    // Fault key (scenario, fault-class, tool-from-scenario) →\n    // distinct models + occurrences + provenance rows.\n    let mut faults: BTreeMap<FaultKey, FaultTally> = BTreeMap::new();\n\n    for row in rows {\n        let kind = row.get(\"kind\").and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str);\n        if kind == Some(\"absent\") {\n            // Absent cells are the record that a cell did not run;\n            // never failures, never dropped (anti-goal — mirrored\n            // from report.rs).\n            continue;\n        }\n        let scenario = row\n            .get(\"scenario\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `scenario` field\")?;\n        let row_tool = row\n            .get(\"tool\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `tool` field\")?;\n        let report_version = row\n            .get(\"report_version\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_u64)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `report_version` field\")?;\n        if report_version != 1 {\n            return Err(format!(\n                \"report row carries report_version {report_version}; routing aggregates version 1 only\"\n            ));\n        }\n        // The repo keys on the scenario definition's recorded tool,\n        // not the row's tool field (hardcoded in live rows; the hhs\n        // bug showed attribution drifts). Unknown scenario → loud.\n        let recorded_tool = scenario_tool_of(scenario).ok_or_else(|| {\n            format!(\"scenario '{scenario}' has no recorded tool — refusing to route\")\n        })?;\n\n[285 more lines in file. Use offset=151 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792429810715},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_870b61bcd0fb4225abeee846","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"//! Purpose: Tier-1/2 report aggregation into the per-channel fault\n//! dashboard (evallerina-48g).\n//! Responsibilities: aggregate report_version 1 rows (tier-2 rotation\n//! events and tier-1 rows without model attribution) into a\n//! per-output-channel × check-code × model-id fault matrix, apply the\n//! evals-guidelines fault routing (tool faults → tool tickets; agent\n//! faults → AIX-channel work only at the cross-model threshold),\n//! surface absent, rate_limited, and http_error cells as coverage\n//! records — never failures, never dropped — and refuse loudly on unmappable scenarios\n//! instead of silently bucketing them.\n//! Rationale: evals-guidelines \"Fault routing\" and \"suite-wide\n//! aggregation\" requirements. The dashboard deliberately carries no\n//! single aggregate score: per-capability breakdown is a hard\n//! requirement, and a collapse into one number hides exactly the\n//! channel × code × model signal the routing rule needs.\n\nuse serde_json::json;\nuse std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};\n\n/// Version of the dashboard shape. Additive evolution only within a\n/// version; breaking changes bump this.\npub const DASHBOARD_VERSION: u32 = 1;\n\n/// The evals-guidelines cross-model routing threshold for agent faults\n/// within one output channel.\nconst ROUTING_THRESHOLD: usize = 3;\n\n/// The output channel a live scenario's checks probe (the fault-routing\n/// key per evals-guidelines: suggestions, envelope, managed-block, or\n/// artifact surface). Registered for every live scenario; the channel\n/// must exist before rotation day, not after the first dashboard run.\npub fn scenario_channel(name: &str) -> Option<&'static str> {\n    match name {\n        \"wai-hint-adherence-smoke\"\n        | \"wai-hint-adherence-corrupt-config\"\n        | \"dont-hint-adherence-lifecycle\" => Some(\"envelope\"),\n        \"wai-hint-adherence-typo\" => Some(\"suggestions\"),\n        \"wai-doc-drift-stale-agents-md\" | \"dont-doc-drift-stale-lifecycle\" => Some(\"artifact\"),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\n/// One accumulator key in the routing maps: agent faults key on\n/// (channel, code), tool faults on (tool, check, channel) because they\n/// carry no code by contract.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]\nenum FaultKey {\n    /// Agent fault: the `ERR_*` taxonomy code within one output channel.\n    Agent { channel: String, code: String },\n    /// Tool fault: no code; routed to the tool's repository.\n    Tool {\n        tool: String,\n        check: String,\n        channel: String,\n    },\n}\n\n/// Accumulated occurrences for one fault key: total count plus the set\n/// of distinct model ids observed (empty for non-tier-2 rows).\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\nstruct FaultTally {\n    occurrences: usize,\n    models: BTreeSet<String>,\n    scenarios: BTreeSet<String>,\n}\n\n/// Aggregate report_version 1 rows into the dashboard JSON.\n///\n/// `rows` are parsed report rows as [`serde_json::Value`]: tier-2\n/// rotation events (`kind: trial | absent`) or tier-1 rows without a\n/// `model` field (grouped as non-tier-2 per the report contract).\n/// `configured_models` is the model registry the rotation was\n/// configured with — it defines the routing threshold (3, or the full\n/// registry size when fewer than three are configured). `channel_of`\n/// resolves a scenario to the output channel its checks probe; an\n/// unknown scenario is a loud error, never a silent bucket.\n///\n/// The returned value is the serialized dashboard shape: no single\n/// aggregate score, per-capability breakdown only.\npub fn aggregate_rows(\n    rows: &[serde_json::Value],\n    configured_models: &[String],\n    channel_of: &dyn Fn(&str) -> Option<&'static str>,\n) -> Result<serde_json::Value, String> {\n    if configured_models.is_empty() {\n        return Err(\"configured model registry is empty — no routing threshold exists\".into());\n    }\n    let routing_threshold = configured_models.len().min(ROUTING_THRESHOLD);\n\n    let mut matrix_cells: BTreeMap<(String, String, String), FaultTally> = BTreeMap::new();\n    let mut routings: BTreeMap<FaultKey, FaultTally> = BTreeMap::new();\n    let mut coverage = Coverage::default();\n    let mut report_versions = BTreeSet::new();\n\n    for row in rows {\n        let kind = row.get(\"kind\").and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str);\n        if kind == Some(\"absent\") {\n            // Absent cells are the record that a cell did not run; they\n            // are never failures and never dropped (anti-goal).\n            coverage.absent += 1;\n            continue;\n        }\n        let status = row\n            .get(\"status\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `status` field\")?;\n        let scenario = row\n            .get(\"scenario\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `scenario` field\")?;\n        let tool = row\n            .get(\"tool\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `tool` field\")?;\n        let checks = row\n            .get(\"checks\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_array)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `checks` array\")?;\n        let report_version = row\n            .get(\"report_version\")\n            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_u64)\n            .ok_or(\"report row lacks a `report_version` field\")?;\n        if report_version != 1 {\n            return Err(format!(\n                \"report row carries report_version {report_version}; this dashboard aggregates version 1 only\"\n            ));\n        }\n        report_versions.insert(report_version);\n\n        let channel = channel_of(scenario).ok_or_else(|| {\n            format!(\"scenario '{scenario}' has no declared output channel — refusing to aggregate\")\n        })?;\n\n        let model = row.get(\"model\").and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str);\n        match model {\n            Some(_) => {\n                coverage.trials += 1;\n                bump_status(&mut coverage, status)?;\n            }\n            None => coverage.non_tier2 += 1,\n        }\n\n        for check in checks {\n            let name = check\n                .get(\"name\")\n                .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_str)\n                .ok_or(\"check row lacks a `name` field\")?;\n            let passed = check\n                .get(\"passed\")\n                .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_bool)\n\n[210 more lines in file. Use offset=151 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792429810715},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b198232c180d43988ca18f89","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":220,"offset":151,"path":"src/report.rs"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bcaa51571cc14a579b7945b9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"propose\\|pub fn\\|repo_map\\|scenario_tool\" src/routing.rs | head -40","timeout":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":3281,"output":66,"cacheRead":11776,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":15123,"cost":{"input":0.00049215,"output":0.000032999999999999996,"cacheRead":0.00035328,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00087843}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792429810716,"responseId":"gen-1792429810-Tp5hvUSaonUCAMOT8GFC","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b198232c180d43988ca18f89","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"                .ok_or(\"check row lacks a `passed` field\")?;\n            if passed {\n                coverage.checks_passed += 1;\n                continue;\n            }\n            coverage.checks_failed += 1;\n       